<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles James Napier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Charles James Napier (1782–1853) was a British general who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 1843. After defeating the Talpur mirs at Miani and Dubba near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>, he annexed the province and governed it until 1847, and he later served as commander-in-chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The story that he announced the conquest with the one-word pun 'Peccavi' ('I have sinned') comes from Punch magazine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-james-napier.jpg" alt="Charles James Napier" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to William Edward Kilburn (1818–91) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_James_Napier_by_William_Edward_Kilburn,_1849-crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
